wowbox64: Wine's cooperative suspend (the CHPE suspend doorbell), as FEX's WoW64 back end

GetThreadContext() of a suspended thread that was running 32-bit x86 code returned the x86 state of the
thread's last call into Wine (its program counter inside Wine's i386 DLLs): SIGUSR1 stopped the thread
somewhere in translated code, and the WoW64 context in the CPU area was the one saved at that call.

Each thread now has a CHPE_V2_CPU_AREA_INFO (the 64-bit TEB's ChpeV2CpuAreaInfo) whose InSimulation is set
while x86 code runs (cleared around system calls, Unix calls and exceptions passed on to the program) and whose
SuspendDoorbell is a new x64emu_t field. Wine's SIGUSR1 handler then rings the doorbell instead of suspending
the thread where it is. The translated code checks it at every block entry, secondary entry and loop head
(every cycle through translated code passes one; two instructions when not rung), the interpreter between
instructions. When it is rung, wow64_suspend_point() gets the x86 state from x64emu_t (the dynarec writes its
cached x87/MMX/SSE registers there first and passes the x86 address and the flags still in NZCV), writes it to
the WoW64 context, leaves simulation and calls NtContinue(): ntdll takes the pending suspend there. The
translated code then goes on unchanged, or, when the suspender changed the context (SetThreadContext), leaves
the block for the new state. Only the WoW64 build (_WIN32) changes; BOX64_DYNAREC_PAUSE=3 (sevl + wfe) falls
back to yield there, as jumps back to a sevl skip the first instruction of the loop head.

